===Etymology 2===
[[File:A PROMINENT PHYSICIAN CHARGED WITH MANSLAUGHTER.jpg|thumb|The lede of this [[article#Noun|article]] entitled “A prominent physician is charged with manslaughter” from ''{{w|The Tacoma Times}}'' ([[Tacoma]], [[Washington]], USA, 11 May 1904) is its [[first]] [[paragraph#Noun|paragraph]].]]

A deliberate misspelling of {{m|en|lead}}, originally used in instructions given to printers to indicate which paragraphs constitute the lede, intended to avoid confusion with the word ''lead'' which may actually appear in the text of an article.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''{{sup|2}}|noformat=1|id=431803|date=March 2019|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref><ref>Alternatively, it has been claimed that the word was misspelled to avoid confusion with {{m|en|lead|t=strip of type metal used for positioning type in the frame}} (pronounced {{IPAchar|/lɛd/}}): see {{cite-web|title=The Maven’s Word of the Day: lede|work={{w|Random House}}|url=http://www.randomhouse.com/wotd/index.pperl?date=20001128|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20010417153423/http://www.randomhouse.com/wotd/index.pperl?date=20001128|archivedate=17 April 2001|date=28 November 2000}}.</ref> Compare {{m|en|dek|t=subhead}} (modified from {{m|en|deck}}) and {{m|en|hed|t=headline}} (from {{m|en|head}}).

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|US|journalism}} The [[introductory]] [[paragraph#Noun|paragraph]] or paragraphs of a [[newspaper]] or other [[news]] [[article#Noun|article]]; the [[lead#Noun|lead]] or [[lead-in]]. {{defdate|from mid 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Michael Tomasky|authorlink=Michael Tomasky|title=Michael Tomasky’s Blog: This Morning’s Stuff You Need to Know|work={{w|The Guardian}}|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20160306082201/http://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/michaeltomasky/2008/oct/15/johnmccain-williamtimmons|archivedate=6 March 2016|location=London|publisher=[[w:Guardian Media Group|Guardian News & Media]]|date=15 October 2008|passage=The '''lede''' (as we spell it) story in today's NYT [''{{w|The New York Times}}''] is all about their new poll showing that [[w:John McCain|[John] McCain]] is hurting himself, not [[w:Barack Obama|[Barack] Obama]], with the attacks. {{...}} If something's the '''lede''' in the NYT, it tends to get discussed on cable TV all day, etc.}}

=====Usage notes=====
The word, which has entered ordinary usage, was originally journalistic jargon. In 1990, the American author and journalist {{w|William Safire}} (1929–2009) was still able to say: “You will not find this spelling in dictionaries; it is still an insiders' variant, steadily growing in frequency of use. {{...}} Will ''lede'' break out of its insider status and find its way into general use? {{...}} To suggest this is becoming standard would be misledeing {{...}} But it has earned its place as a variant spelling, soon to overtake the original spelling for the beginning of a news article.”<ref>{{cite-journal|en|author=William Safire|authorlink=William Safire|title=On language: (HED) folo my lede (UNHED)|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1990/11/18/magazine/on-language-hed-folo-my-lede-unhed.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210703040610/https://www.nytimes.com/1990/11/18/magazine/on-language-hed-folo-my-lede-unhed.html|archivedate=3 July 2021|magazine={{w|The New York Times Magazine}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|The New York Times Company}}|date=18 November 1990|section=section 6|page=22|issn=0028-7822|oclc=762261046}}.</ref>

